From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 04:14:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08025/11362] The search's dedup key was 38% of its time; the
 obvious way to fix that is wrong

`plan_in_model` called `to_ascii` once per engine call to test whether it had
already seen a state. A cProfile of a shipped-budget ka59 search put that single
function at 38% of the whole search -- ~1.3M `str.join` calls building a
one-char-per-cell Python string. The search is CPU-bound replay under a hard
per-game wall-clock budget, so that 38% comes straight out of nodes explored.

The swap looks trivial. It is not, and the interesting part of this change is
why:

  `to_ascii` renders each cell as `str(int(v))[-1]` -- the LAST DIGIT ONLY. So it
  MERGES colour 4 with 14, 5 with 15, 1 with 11, 0 with 10. The shipped key is
  LOSSY. A plain `g.tobytes()` distinguishes those states, which is a strictly
  FINER partition: a change to which states the search explores, not a speedup.

That is measured, not argued. Across ten games a plain-bytes key changes the
search on cn04 -- 93 engine calls / 9 unique states becomes 140 / 14. Shipped as
"a faster key" it would have altered search behaviour while every count a casual
check looks at stayed plausible, and the aliasing colours are in the ROOT grids
of ka59, lp85, cn04, sp80, sc25, sk48, tu93 and re86, so the exposure was
corpus-wide rather than a cn04 curiosity.

What shipped is `_state_key`: `(a % 10).astype(np.uint8).tobytes()` behind a
shape prefix, on a fast path guarded to 2-D, integer, NON-NEGATIVE grids, with
every other input deferred to `to_ascii` itself so equivalence holds by
construction rather than by argument. Swapped in at all 5 key sites.

AN ADVERSARIAL REVIEW FOUND A REAL BUG IN THIS BEFORE IT LANDED, and it is
recorded rather than quietly fixed because all eight tests I had written passed
against it. The first version took the arithmetic path for ANY integer grid,
while the docstring, the spec, the ops docs and every test asserted that `% 10`
matched `to_ascii` "for every integer, negatives included". False: `to_ascii`
takes the last character of the DECIMAL STRING, i.e. the last digit of the
ABSOLUTE value -- `str(-1)[-1] == "1"` -- whereas `-1 % 10 == 9`. They agree only
where a digit is its own complement mod 10, so they disagree on 12 of the 16
values in -15..-1, and `-1` and `9` (distinct states under `to_ascii`) would have
been merged into one. Every test passed because every test used non-negative
colours, which is what real ARC grids contain -- exactly the "tests test what the
author thought to test" mode CLAUDE.md's QA-Layer discipline names, and the
second instance of it in this session after REQ-ARC-WMTE-6050. Practical exposure
on the measured corpus was ZERO (all root grids are non-negative, the fast path
was taken throughout, no measured number moved), so the bug was LATENT -- which is
why it survived. `np.abs(a) % 10` would have matched everywhere except at a
dtype's most negative value, where `abs` silently overflows; declining negatives
is the boring choice and it is the right one.

The same review found the bulk test's anti-vacuity guard satisfied by exactly the
case it was written to exclude: 60 random grids give 1830 pairs with 60
collisions, and all 60 are SELF-pairs (measured: 0 non-trivial collisions). So
"collides where it collides" was only ever tested by comparing a grid with
itself. The corpus is now constructed with aliasing-perturbed twins and both
directions are asserted to hold over >=30 non-trivial pairs.

VERIFICATION, and why it is not a count comparison. Partition identity is proved
by ACCEPT TRACE: one entry per engine call, in order, for what the search DECIDED
(accepted / duplicate / shape-skip / engine raised), hashed. That sequence is
key-INDEPENDENT, so equal hashes mean the identical search ran; equal
`engine_calls` and `unique_states` would not, since two different partitions can
coincidentally agree on both.

  10 games at a 20,000-engine-call cap: partition-identical on all ten.
  Speedup on the four whose engines do enough work to time:
    ka59 (engine pinned at 341f776c9) 1.28x
    sk48                              4.59x
    sc25                              6.61x
    lp85                              7.18x
  No regression anywhere; minimum 1.28x.

Evidence weight is NOT uniform and the docs say so: only those four ran to the
cap (887-1,994 states each). The other six exhaust their queue in 1-148 calls
because their current on-disk engines are near-inert, so their agreement is real
but close to vacuous. They are still reported, because the one game where the
plain-bytes key diverges is itself a 93-call game. The speed spread is explained
rather than blended: ka59's peak engine is expensive (`_blocks` alone was 48% of
its search) so the key is a smaller share of its total.

Two measurement errors of my own, found and corrected mid-flight rather than
shipped: the first harness ran the control arm first and reported "371x" on ka59,
which was one-off engine warm-up charged to the control (it now discards a warm-up
run and takes the min of two timed reps); and the evidence artifact's first draft
declared the MEASUREMENT's substrate while carrying the BUILDER's 0.0019s runtime,
which `adversarial_verify` flagged CRITICAL DURATION_TOO_SHORT -- the
REQ-ARC-WMTE-6050 check landed hours earlier in this same session, firing on its
own author. The substrate was corrected rather than the duration massaged, the raw
per-arm rows are committed as cited upstream evidence, and the artifact's headline
verdict is now DERIVED from those rows because the hand-written one had already
gone stale (it still read "1_25x_to_7_27x" after the key was corrected).

Test-suite state, stated rather than implied: the ARC-related subset is 53 failed,
8975 passed, 13 skipped, 1 error. Those 53 are PRE-EXISTING -- the identical subset
was re-run against the pre-swap file and the failure sets are byte-identical, zero
new and zero fixed, with the +8 passed accounted for exactly by the new tests. They
are overwhelmingly artifact-schema assertions, not search behaviour. 53 red tests
plus 13 skips is a standing finding in its own right (skips are invisible failures
per CLAUDE.md); neither is this change's to fix.

NOT claimed closed: the affordability gap is untouched. A ka59 plan needs 137,347
engine calls and the conservative per-game non-LLM budget affords ~17,854, so a
1.28x key win on that game does not close a ~7.7x gap -- the remaining lever is a
smaller BRANCHING FACTOR (18 candidates per expansion, 90.2% of engine calls
landing on an already-seen grid), not a faster key and not a better frontier order.
Separately, the lossy last-digit merge is now documented and REPRODUCED, not fixed;
whether the search should distinguish colour 4 from 14 is a behaviour change owed
its own change and its own evidence.

