+    },
+    "n_games_with_usable_timing": 4,
+    "min_speedup": 1.2828,
+    "no_regression_on_any_timed_game": true,
+    "excluded_from_the_speed_table_and_why": {
+      "cn04": "control ended queue_exhausted after 93 calls in 0.048s -- too little work to time",
+      "ar25": "control ended queue_exhausted after 16 calls in 0.005s -- too little work to time",
+      "m0r0": "control ended plan_found after 1 calls in 0.003s -- too little work to time",
+      "sp80": "control ended queue_exhausted after 56 calls in 0.025s -- too little work to time",
+      "tu93": "control ended queue_exhausted after 148 calls in 0.052s -- too little work to time",
+      "re86": "control ended queue_exhausted after 28 calls in 0.01s -- too little work to time"
+    },
+    "why_the_spread_is_not_averaged": "ka59's peak engine is expensive -- a single LLM-written helper (_blocks) was 48% of its search -- so the key is a smaller share of ka59's total than of games with cheap engines. Reporting one blended figure would hide that the lever's size depends on the engine, which is the thing a reader needs in order to predict it elsewhere."
+  },
+  "MEASUREMENT_HONESTY": {
+    "arm_order_artefact_found_and_fixed": "The first version of this measurement ran the control arm first and reported a '371x speedup' on ka59. That was one-off engine warm-up charged entirely to the control, not speed. Every arm is now preceded by a discarded warm-up run and timed twice with the MINIMUM taken. The corrected ka59 figure is 1.25x.",
+    "engine_pinning": "ka59 is measured against the engine at git 341f776c9, the one the original 137,347-call profile used. Today's on-disk ka59 engine exhausts its queue in 36 calls, so citing it as 'ka59' would have compared a different program.",
+    "cap_semantics": "Every arm runs to the SAME 20,000-engine-call cap, so wall clock compares equal work and the partition comparison covers the whole explored prefix rather than stopping at the first goal state.",
+    "what_this_does_NOT_show": "It does not show the search now fits the per-game budget, and it does not show the lossy merge is harmless -- only that the swap did not change it. The affordability question and the merge question are both still open."
